WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.960
Three places in a payment service call something flaky: the card issuer, the ledger, and the FX rate service.

2
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.838
Each call sits inside the same loop: try up to three times, then give up.

3
00:00:14.838 --> 00:00:17.125
Only the call in the middle changes.

4
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:21.149
How do you write that loop once, when the part that changes is a call?

5
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:24.927
Pass the call in, as a function.

6
00:00:24.927 --> 00:00:26.298
retry owns the loop.

7
00:00:26.298 --> 00:00:30.412
The call arrives as block, and retry decides when to run it.

8
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:32.768
Here the issuer times out twice.

9
00:00:32.768 --> 00:00:37.239
retry runs block again, and the third try returns an authorization.

10
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:40.072
The ledger call plugs into the same socket.

11
00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:43.683
A function that takes a function is a higher-order function.

12
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:46.242
It works the other way too.

13
00:00:46.242 --> 00:00:49.693
feeFor takes a country, and hands back a fee function.

14
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.899
It looks up the Dutch rate once.

15
00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:56.752
The function it returns keeps that number, and prices every payment after that.

16
00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:00.179
Small functions also snap together.

17
00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:04.850
Add a 25-cent scheme fee, take 10 percent off, cap at 10 euros.

18
00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:07.591
Feed each result into the next.

19
00:01:07.591 --> 00:01:12.687
That's composition: three functions become one pipeline, price.

20
00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:15.194
1,200 cents comes out as 1,000.

21
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:17.481
Move the cap to the front.

22
00:01:17.481 --> 00:01:18.348
Same price?

23
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:20.291
No.

24
00:01:20.291 --> 00:01:20.768
922.

25
00:01:20.768 --> 00:01:24.583
The order is part of the result.

26
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:27.866
In code, the call goes in as a lambda.

27
00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:30.252
Java's Function has andThen built in.

28
00:01:30.252 --> 00:01:34.055
Kotlin's function types don't, so you write it in one line.

29
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:40.565
The rule: write the shape of the work once, and pass in the part that changes as a function.

30
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.655
Quick check.

31
00:01:41.655 --> 00:01:44.863
timed runs a block and logs how long it took.

32
00:01:44.863 --> 00:01:46.217
Is it higher-order?

33
00:01:47.817 --> 00:01:48.143
Yes.

34
00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:50.838
It takes the block as a function.
